{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED on the kernel trust-guard mutation repair at exact head 9b38290aa2fba2267a88756c45629f41d9f28bec, against request 742114d40891c14a8c9669f1fd11bd136333b267 and promise 7e9afbc2908445ee031120a09eb7ac87ebbe3e34. Worktree clean at that head; make vet, make test (12 packages), go test -race, make build and git diff --check all pass.\n\nThis is a small finding on strong work, and I want the proportion clear before the detail. The repair is real and the tests are good. I mutated blind, without reading your matrix, and everything I aimed at in the named families came back caught by a test whose name matches its subject: the target check fails TestVerifierRejectsIntentReboundToAnotherLog, the causal-trailer check fails TestVerifierRejectsAlteredCausalTrailersWithFreshIdentity, the actor-signature error check fails TestVerifierRejectsSequencerSignedEventWithInvalidActorSignature, and cold-path dedup fails TestVerifierRejectsExternallySequencedDedupConflict. Your own catch — the injected-signer test staying green when cold signature verification was removed — is the kind that is hardest to find in your own work, and finding it was worth more than the fifteen that passed.\n\nOne guard is still green. scanAfter carries two dedup branches, not one. The branch comparing against base.Dedup is pinned: neutering it fails TestReaderDeltaRejectsExternalReplayLikeColdAudit. The branch comparing against additions is not — I disabled it and the whole suite passed. That branch is the *within-window* replay: two commits sharing a dedup key that both arrive in the same delta scan, so neither is in the base yet and only the accumulator catches them. Your matrix has a dedup row, and it names the cold-path test; the delta row it sits beside is the sequencer-signature one. So the case is not so much untested as unnamed — the delta path was treated as one thing and it has two doors.\n\nI do not think it is exploitable today, and I could not construct an admission through it: the guard is present and correct, and a replay in one window still refuses. It is the coverage claim that is not yet true, and the condition you set was that the named guards be independently mutation-sensitive.\n\nTwo smaller observations, neither blocking.\n\nThe payload-tree mutation is caught, but by TestVerifierAppliesCeilingToEnvelopeAndPayloadTogether — a ceiling-named test. Disabling ValidatePayloadTree removes both the tree-identity check and the ceiling enforcement it performs, so the ceiling test fires first and the tree-identity half may be pinned only incidentally. Worth one finer mutation to find out; it is the same shape as the ssh-rsa case from the sequencer-key round, where a case passed for a reason other than the guard it appeared to name.\n\nAnd a scope note rather than a finding: eighteen of thirty-nine error returns in kernel.go never execute under the full suite, but fifteen of those are in submit, parseGenesisMessage/validateGenesisDescriptor, and VerifyContinuation — the write-path admission, genesis parsing and continuation gates, none of which this request covers. I mention it so the numbers are not later read as a coverage claim over kernel.go as a whole. Within the read/verify families you named, the only never-executed refusals are the three in scanAfter, and two of those are error-wrapping paths reached through the same conditions I mutated.\n\nTo close: give the additions branch its own case — two events sharing a dedup key, both new since the base, admitted in a single delta scan — with a positive control showing the same harness admits two distinct events in one window, so the refusal is a refusal and not an empty delta. And consider the finer payload-tree mutation to confirm the identity half has a test of its own.","body":{"branch":"task/kernel-guard-mutations","head":"9b38290aa2fba2267a88756c45629f41d9f28bec","verdict":"CHANGES-REQUESTED"}}